        </p>

        <TopPick />

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Short Answer
          </h2>

          <p>
            Choose carbon fiber if you want control, consistency and spin. Carbon faces are stiff, so the face deflects less at impact and the ball goes where the paddle points. Raw carbon faces, with their matte, slightly gritty texture, also grip the ball better, which is why most spin-focused paddles use them. The trade-off is price, and a slightly less forgiving, less bouncy feel on soft swings.
          </p>

          <p>
            Choose fiberglass if you want easy pop and a lower price. Fiberglass, sometimes called composite, flexes more than carbon, so the face acts a little like a trampoline and returns energy to the ball. That makes drives and put-aways feel effortless, which many newer and power-oriented players love. The downsides are less precise control on touch shots and, on smooth faces, less spin.
          </p>

          <p>
            Hybrid faces mix the two, and graphite is a thin, stiff carbon-family face found on lighter and older-style paddles. Kevlar and other aramid blends show up on premium paddles that promise durability and a distinctive feel. Each sits somewhere on the same spectrum between stiff and controlled at one end and flexible and poppy at the other.
          </p>

          <p>
            Face material is only one part of the equation. Core thickness, construction and weight all interact with it. A fiberglass face on a 16mm core can feel controlled, and a carbon face on a thermoformed 14mm core can be very fast. Our <a href="/best-pickleball-paddle/">best pickleball paddle picks</a> list face material alongside core and construction so you can compare whole paddles, not just faces.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Carbon for control and spin, fiberglass for pop and price.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">If you are unsure, a raw carbon face on a 16mm core is the most forgiving all-round setup for doubles players.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="materials" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. What the Face Materials Actually Are
          </h2>

          <p>
            Carbon fiber faces are made from woven or unidirectional carbon fibers set in resin. Manufacturers often name the grade, with Toray T700 the most commonly advertised. T700 is a standard-modulus aerospace and sporting-goods fiber from the Japanese maker Toray, and its popularity on paddles comes from a good balance of stiffness, strength and cost. Some paddles quote other grades or layups, and the exact construction matters as much as the fiber name.
          </p>

          <p>
            Raw carbon fiber means the face has not been coated with a smooth paint or glossy clear layer, leaving the fiber texture exposed. Many raw carbon paddles also add grit or a textured topcoat. That texture is the main reason raw carbon paddles spin the ball well. USA Pickleball limits surface roughness for sanctioned paddles, so textures must stay within the rules to be approved.
          </p>

          <p>
            Fiberglass faces use glass fibers in resin. Glass is more flexible than carbon at the same thickness, which gives the face its trampoline effect. Fiberglass paddles are often painted with bright graphics and tend to sit at lower price points. Some fiberglass faces have textured coatings for spin, but most are smoother than raw carbon.
          </p>

          <p>
            Graphite faces are a thin, stiff carbon-based layer. In practice, graphite paddles feel similar to carbon ones, crisp and controlled, and were common on lighter paddles before raw carbon became dominant. Kevlar and aramid faces, and hybrids that layer carbon with fiberglass or aramid, aim to combine durability, power and feel. They are usually priced at the premium end.
          </p>

