        <div id="feel" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Stiffness, Pop and Feel
          </h2>

          <p>
            Stiffness is the key difference. A stiff carbon face deflects very little, so the ball's departure angle closely matches the paddle angle. That precision is why carbon paddles are favoured by players who want their dinks, drops and resets to land exactly where they aimed. It also makes carbon feel solid and consistent across the face.
          </p>

          <p>
            Fiberglass deflects more. When the ball hits, the face flexes and springs back, adding a little speed. Players feel that as pop: drives jump off the face and put-aways need less effort. On touch shots, the same flex makes the ball slightly harder to control, because a little extra energy comes back even when you want none.
          </p>

          <p>
            Feel is partly subjective. Some players describe carbon as dead or muted, especially paired with a thick core; others call it connected. Some describe fiberglass as lively; others call it springy and unpredictable. If you can demo both, hit soft dinks and hard blocks with each, because those are the shots where the difference shows most clearly.
          </p>

          <p>
            Core thickness amplifies or softens these traits. A fiberglass face on a 14mm core is about as poppy as non-thermoformed paddles get, while a carbon face on a 16mm core is about as controlled. Our <a href="/14mm-vs-16mm-pickleball-paddle/">14mm vs 16mm guide</a> explains the core side of the trade, and our <a href="/thermoformed-pickleball-paddle/">thermoformed paddle guide</a> covers how modern construction adds power to carbon faces.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="spin" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Spin and Surface Texture
          </h2>

          <p>
            Spin comes from friction between the ball and the face while they are in contact, combined with the angle and speed of your swing. A rougher face grips the ball better, so for the same swing it imparts more rotation. That is why raw carbon, with its exposed texture or added grit, leads the spin charts in most reviews.
          </p>

          <p>
            Material and texture work together. Carbon's stiffness helps spin indirectly, because a stable face keeps the contact consistent. Fiberglass can spin well if it has a textured coating, but many fiberglass paddles have smooth painted faces that grip the ball less. If spin matters to you, check whether the face is raw or textured before you check the material name.
          </p>

          <p>
            Texture wears. Grit and raw textures gradually smooth out with play, especially on outdoor courts and with harder outdoor balls. Many players report that a raw carbon paddle loses some of its bite over months of regular use. Cleaning the face with a damp cloth or a paddle eraser can restore some grip by removing ball residue, but worn texture does not come back. Our <a href="/how-long-do-pickleball-paddles-last/">paddle lifespan guide</a> explains the signs.
          </p>

          <p>
            Rules cap how much texture is allowed. USA Pickleball sets limits on surface roughness and has removed paddles from its approved list over surface texture in the past. If you play sanctioned events, choose a model on the current approved list; our <a href="/usa-pickleball-approved-paddles/">approved paddles guide</a> explains how to check. For spin-focused picks, see our <a href="/best-pickleball-paddle-for-spin/">best paddles for spin</a>.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">SPIN CHECK</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Look for raw or textured carbon first.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Face texture drives spin more than the fiber name. A smooth painted carbon face can spin less than a textured fiberglass one.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="durability" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Durability and Wear
          </h2>

          <p>
            Carbon fiber faces are strong and resist dents well, but their raw textures wear down with play. That means a carbon paddle may stay structurally sound long after it has lost some spin. Edge chips and cracked edge guards are more common failure points than the face itself on most paddles.
          </p>

          <p>
            Fiberglass faces are tough too, though their flexibility and painted graphics can show scuffs and chips sooner. Because the face flexes more, the bond between face and core takes more cyclical stress. Delamination, where the face separates from the core and a soft or dead spot appears, can happen with any material, but players often report it sooner on cheaper paddles regardless of face.
          </p>

          <p>
            Core crush is a separate issue. With heavy use, the honeycomb under the most-hit area can compress, creating a dead spot that feels hollow and sounds different. This affects both carbon and fiberglass paddles and depends more on core quality, construction and how hard you hit than on face material. Thermoformed paddles have their own reported durability questions, covered in our <a href="/thermoformed-pickleball-paddle/">thermoformed guide</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            For durability, treat the warranty as a signal. Better paddles often come with longer warranties, and some cover delamination and core issues explicitly. Store your paddle away from extreme heat, such as a hot car, which can soften resins and adhesives, and use a cover to protect the face and edge.
          </p>

          <p>
            Edge guards deserve attention on both materials. A cracked or loose edge guard lets moisture and grit reach the core, and repeated ground scrapes on low balls wear it quickly. Check the edge after each session, replace worn guards where the brand allows it, and avoid tapping the paddle on the court between points, which is one of the most common sources of edge damage.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="price" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Price and Value
          </h2>

          <p>
            Fiberglass paddles dominate the budget end. You can find well-made fiberglass paddles at low prices that play well for newer players, and many beginner sets use fiberglass faces. If you are just starting out or buying paddles for casual family play, fiberglass offers the best value.
          </p>

          <p>
            Carbon fiber paddles range from affordable to premium. Raw carbon paddles have come down in price as the technology spread, and several mid-priced models now offer raw T700 faces and decent textures. Premium carbon paddles add thermoforming, foam-injected edges, special layups and longer warranties.
          </p>

          <p>
            The value question is whether you will use what carbon offers. If you already control the ball well and want more spin and precision, carbon is worth it. If you mainly want to get the ball over the net and enjoy the game, a good fiberglass paddle does that for less money and may even feel more fun because of its pop.
          </p>

          <p>
            Avoid paying for features you cannot use yet. A premium thermoformed carbon paddle can amplify both good and bad habits. Many coaches suggest newer players start with a forgiving, mid-priced paddle and upgrade once they know their style. Our <a href="/how-to-choose-a-pickleball-paddle/">guide to choosing a paddle</a> walks through that decision.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Typical Ranges, Not Test Results</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">T700</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Most advertised carbon grade</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">Raw</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Face type that spins most</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">Fiberglass</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Most common budget face</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">Months</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Typical time before grit wears noticeably</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="choosing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Which Face to Choose
          </h2>

          <p>
            Newer players on a budget should start with fiberglass or an affordable carbon paddle with a 16mm core. Both are forgiving enough to learn on. If you already know you love touch shots, choose the carbon; if you love hitting hard, choose the fiberglass.
          </p>

          <p>
            Intermediate players who want to add spin, especially topspin drives and rolls, should move to raw carbon. The texture and stiffness make spin far easier to produce, and the control helps resets. Pair it with the core thickness that suits your misses.
          </p>

          <p>
            Power players who want effortless pace without a big swing often prefer fiberglass or a thermoformed carbon paddle. Players with arm pain usually do better with a thicker core and a slightly heavier paddle regardless of face, and our <a href="/best-pickleball-paddle-for-tennis-elbow/">tennis elbow picks</a> reflect that.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whatever you choose, demo if you can, and buy from a seller with a reasonable return or trial policy. Then compare specific models in our <a href="/best-pickleball-paddle/">best pickleball paddle rankings</a>, where we list face material, texture, core and weight for each pick.
          </p>

          <p>
            Players who switch faces should expect a short adjustment period. Moving from fiberglass to carbon usually means drives land a little shorter and touch shots feel more precise; moving the other way means more pace with less effort but a few extra balls that float. Give a new face several sessions before judging it, and keep the core thickness the same during the switch if you want a fair comparison.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying on fiber name alone</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Labels like T700 tell you the fiber grade, not how the paddle plays. Texture, layup, core thickness and construction matter as much. Compare whole paddles by feel, spin and control, and treat the fiber name as one data point rather than a guarantee of performance.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting spin from a smooth face</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Spin depends heavily on surface texture. A smooth, glossy carbon face can spin less than a textured fiberglass face. If spin is your goal, look for raw or textured faces specifically, and check that the model is on the approved list if you play sanctioned events.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring texture wear</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Raw and gritty textures smooth out with use, especially outdoors. A paddle that spun well when new may spin less after months of play. Clean the face regularly, expect gradual change, and consider replacement when spin drops noticeably rather than blaming your technique.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Overbuying as a beginner</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Premium thermoformed carbon paddles can amplify mistakes for newer players. A forgiving, mid-priced paddle often helps you learn faster. Upgrade once you know whether you are a touch player or a power player, so the new paddle supports your style.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving the paddle in a hot car</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                High heat can soften resins and adhesives in any paddle, contributing to delamination and dead spots. Store your paddle indoors or in an insulated bag, use a cover, and avoid leaving it in direct sun or a closed car on hot days.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is carbon fiber better than fiberglass for pickleball?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Neither is better for everyone. Carbon fiber offers more control and, when raw or textured, more spin. Fiberglass offers more pop and usually costs less. Players who value precision and spin generally prefer carbon; players who want easy power on a budget often prefer fiberglass.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is T700 carbon fiber?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                T700 is a standard-modulus carbon fiber made by Toray and widely used in sporting goods. It is popular on paddles because it balances stiffness, strength and cost. The name tells you the fiber grade, but the paddle's layup, texture and core also shape how it plays, so compare whole paddles.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does fiberglass give more power?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Generally yes. Fiberglass faces flex more than carbon, creating a trampoline effect that returns energy to the ball. That makes drives feel effortless. Thermoformed carbon paddles can also be very powerful through their construction, so face material is not the only route to pace.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is graphite the same as carbon fiber?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                They are related. Graphite faces are thin, stiff carbon-based layers, and they feel similar to carbon fiber: crisp and controlled. In paddle marketing, raw carbon fiber usually refers to textured woven or unidirectional carbon faces, while graphite often appears on lighter or older-style designs.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why do raw carbon paddles lose spin?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Raw and gritty textures wear down with use, especially outdoors with harder balls and rough courts. Ball residue can also fill the texture. Cleaning helps remove residue, but worn texture does not regenerate. Many players notice reduced spin after months of regular play.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are Kevlar pickleball paddles worth it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Kevlar and aramid faces target durability and a distinctive feel, and they usually sit at premium prices. They can be worth it for players who play often and want a long-lasting face. For most recreational players, a good raw carbon or fiberglass paddle offers better value.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which face is best for beginners?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A fiberglass face or an affordable carbon face on a 16mm core suits most beginners. Fiberglass helps players who want easy power; carbon helps players who enjoy touch shots. Both are forgiving enough to learn on, and neither requires a premium price.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

